# 31 - Audit Columns + Batch ID (Delta Practical) (Real DE Work)

This practical covers:
- Why audit columns are required
- created_at, updated_at
- load_date
- batch_id
- pipeline_run_id concept
- best practices

Audit columns are mandatory in real pipelines.

---

## 1) Why Audit Columns?

Audit columns help you:
✅ track when record was inserted  
✅ track last update  
✅ debug pipeline runs  
✅ support incremental loads  
✅ build monitoring  

---

## 2) Create Sample Source Data

```python
from pyspark.sql.functions import current_timestamp, lit, to_date

batch_id = "batch_2026_02_07_01"
load_date = "2026-02-07"

data = [
    (1, "Pramod", "IT", 50000),
    (2, "Akash", "HR", 60000),
    (3, "Rahul", "IT", 45000),
]

df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ["id", "name", "dept", "salary"])
```

---

## 3) Add Audit Columns

```python
df_audit = df     .withColumn("created_at", current_timestamp())     .withColumn("updated_at", current_timestamp())     .withColumn("load_date", to_date(lit(load_date)))     .withColumn("batch_id", lit(batch_id))

df_audit.show(truncate=False)
df_audit.printSchema()
```

---

## 4) Write to Delta Table

```python
delta_path = "/tmp/delta/audit/employees"

df_audit.write.format("delta")     .mode("overwrite")     .partitionBy("load_date")     .save(delta_path)
```

---

## 5) Incremental Batch Example (New Batch)

```python
batch_id2 = "batch_2026_02_08_01"
load_date2 = "2026-02-08"

data2 = [
    (2, "Akash", "HR", 65000),  # update
    (4, "Neha", "Finance", 70000)
]

df2 = spark.createDataFrame(data2, ["id", "name", "dept", "salary"])     .withColumn("created_at", current_timestamp())     .withColumn("updated_at", current_timestamp())     .withColumn("load_date", to_date(lit(load_date2)))     .withColumn("batch_id", lit(batch_id2))
```

Append:

```python
df2.write.format("delta")     .mode("append")     .partitionBy("load_date")     .save(delta_path)
```

---

## 6) Validate

```python
spark.read.format("delta").load(delta_path).show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 7) Best Practices

✅ Always store batch_id in silver tables  
✅ Keep created_at and updated_at  
✅ Partition by load_date  
✅ Use MERGE for updates  
✅ Use batch_id for debugging and monitoring  

---

## 8) Summary

- Audit columns are essential.
- batch_id helps track pipeline run.
- load_date supports partitioning.
- created_at/updated_at supports history.

